```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "index of x after setting is more than or same as zero");
```

The above C# code is derived from the provided C# code. The assertion is based on the state of index of x after the test. If the index of x after the test is not greater than or equal to zero, the assertion will fail. The assertion message "index of x after setting is more than or same as zero" will appear in the output during the execution if the test fails.--------------------The regular expression you're looking for is "\/\*\*\(.*\)\s*@@@ (.*)". This regular expression extracts the first line of the comment that starts with "@@@" and captures the text after this string.

Here's a Python code that uses "re" module to extract comments corresponding to "@@@"

In order to keep these examples general, I'm using Python but this solution should be readily usable in other languages.

```python
import re

# define the variable 
code = """
\/\*\*\(.*\)\s*@@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
\/\*\*\(.*\)\s*@@@ array list should have less count (precondition)
\/\*\*\(.*\)\s*@@@ array list should have more count (post condition)
"""
 
matches = re.findall(r'\/\*\*\(.*\)\s*@@@ (.*)', code)

# output the matches
for match in matches:
    print(match)
```

This code will yield the following:

```
index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
array list should have less count (precondition)
array list should have more count (post condition)
```

This gives you the statements that start with "@@@". In your case, you can replace the "code" variable with the string containing your CSharp code and it will perform the same result.--------------------```cs
// We have to use this particular statement because we are expecting the list size to increase after setting an element
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount > Old_arrListCount);

// We also expect an index of x to be the same as the provided one after setting the value into the list
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index);

// We want to confirm this, 'x' still appears in the last index of the list, after setting the value
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// It is also expected that after setting the value, 'x' should still be present in the list as there's no insertion or deletion of 'x' from the list
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);

// Finally, we are expecting to see a different behaviour in the form of index of 'x' after setting. It should be more than or same as zero (inclusive)
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);

```

Note: The Debug.Assert statement will only work if the DEBUG configuration is selected when running the application, hence ensure this before running the test.--------------------The assert statement in the given code checks whether after setting a value at a certain index in ArrayList, the IndexOf(x) method still returns a value equal to or greater than zero. Here is the corresponding csharp code using Debug.Assert:

