Note that the assertion is made about a potentially increasing value. If the array list index of the element 'x' is negative or increased, this indicates that maybe the order of the elements has been changed. This assertion might also be false if arrList already contains 'x' in the index that we are setting to 'x'. In such case, the method might not change the count of 'x' in arrList.--------------------Here is the output assert statements corresponding to the comments that use "@ @ @". 

```cs
// @@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero
Debug.Assert((New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0), "Unexpected index of x.");

// @@@ last index of x is same as last index of x before setting
Debug.Assert((New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX), "Unexpected last index of x.");

// @@@ count of list is same as count before setting
Debug.Assert((New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount), "Unexpected count.");

// @@@ contains is same as before setting
