Important note: The code above uses "Debug.Assert" for checking conditions, but be aware that usage of "Debug.Assert" may affect performance. Do not use assertions to validate normal processing, only to help catch errors during development and testing.--------------------The code that you are looking for can be found below:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "index of x after setting is more than or same as zero");
```

Explanation:
In the `PUT_SetContract` method, we are setting a new value at the specified index in the `arrList`. The new `index` of `x` (when x is in the list) is stored in `New_arrListIndexOfX`. We are then using the `Debug.Assert` statement to check whether `New_arrListIndexOfX` is greater than or equal to zero. The condition is set to fail if the index of x is less than 0, indicating an error in our code. The failure message "index of x after setting is more than or same as zero" will be displayed if the `Debug.Assert` statement gets triggered.--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```--------------------The following C# code corresponds to the comment "@@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero".

```cs
System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "The index of x after setting is less than zero");
```

In the above code, we are asserting the truthiness of the statement `New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0`. If the statement evaluates to `false`, the debugger will break into action and display an error message "The index of x after setting is less than zero". 

The complete C# code for the whole method is:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // @@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "The index of x after setting is less than zero");
        }
    }
}

```--------------------The following are C# debug assert statements based on the comment in the above provided code:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "index of x after setting is less than zero");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_index, "last index of x is not same as old index");
```

"index of x after setting is less than zero" is a failure scenario as the new index of `x` (`New_arrListIndexOfX`) should be same or more than zero.

"last index of x is not same as old index" is a failure scenario as we are assuming that `index` to be at `Old_index` and updating the value. Therefore, after setting the value, last index of `x` should be same as `Old_index`.--------------------